          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "NODES\n\nNodes are the building blocks of your patch.\nNodes create textures, hold values, receive user input, generated data, modulate signals and much more.\n\nIn this tutorial we'll teach the fundamentals of working with nodes."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 154.17527770996094,
          "width": 758.79248046875,
          "x": 207.603759765625,
          "y": 190.94607543945312
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffd0c117",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "1": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "INPUT & OUTPUT\n\nNodes have their inputs on the left side and outputs on the right side.\nThese are sometimes referred to as inlets and outlets.\n\nThe inlets and outlets are represented by symbols like circles, rectangles or diamonds.\nThe type of symbol indicates what kind of data flow is used (more on that later).\n\nHovering over an inlet or outlet will preview the value (if any) and type.\nTry hovering over the outlet of the Sine node below.\nYou will see the value moving and the data type (Float).\n\nNow try hovering over the inlets.\nYou will see the value and multiple data types (Float, Integer and Bool).\nThis simply means that the inlet can accept these data types."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 556.177490234375,
          "width": 647.7354125976562,
          "x": 207.3282165527344,
          "y": 389.7085266113281
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffff6a00",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "10": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "INSERTING & EXPOSING\n\nYou can insert a node into an existing connection by double clicking on the cord.\n\nAssignment: insert a Colorize node between the Texture In and Texture Out nodes.\n\nYou can easily create input nodes by exposing a parameter.\nTo expose a parameter right click on the inlet you want to expose and select 'expose'.\n\nAssignment: expose the color parameter on the Colorize node."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 460.8599853515625,
          "width": 696.0419921875,
