      "2": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "FLOW\n\nEach node has one of three types of data flow: Signal, Event or Attribute.\n\nSignal is the most commonly used and indicates that the data is being outputted at the current framerate.\nFor example: the Metaballs node needs to send its output to the screen every frame for us to see it, so it uses signal flow.\n\nEvent flow is used when something needs to happen upon a change or a trigger. \nFor example: the Metronome node sends out a trigger at a given frequency, so it needs event flow to do this.\n\nAttribute flow is used when a certain value needs to be calculated only once.\nFor example: Pi uses attribute flow as it is a constant value that never changes.\nThere is a lot more to this flow type, but we'll cover that in a separate tutorial."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 556.385498046875,
          "width": 849.4119873046875,
          "x": 883.2402954101562,
          "y": 389.9025573730469
